1357
01:22:03,190 --> 01:22:09,534
[SPEAKER_01]: It occurred on Thursday afternoon, June 3rd, 1982.

1358
01:22:09,634 --> 01:22:11,455
[SPEAKER_01]: This was the day before the

1359
01:22:12,152 --> 01:22:16,775
[SPEAKER_01]: NCAA Division III World Series commenced here in Marietta out of Pioneer Park.

1360
01:22:17,376 --> 01:22:23,860
[SPEAKER_01]: Lefty Gomez was a guest speaker at the banquet preceding this tournament on that Thursday evening.

1361
01:22:24,461 --> 01:22:32,786
[SPEAKER_01]: And he flew into town on the Wednesday night, was out to the ballpark in the morning, and it was arranged that I should have him for a couple hours on Thursday afternoon.

1362
01:22:32,846 --> 01:22:41,032
[SPEAKER_01]: So I picked him up at the hotel, brought him and his friend, another representative from the Wilson Sporting Goods Company, over to the house,

1363
01:22:41,794 --> 01:22:47,299
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, he got here about 2.30 and stayed about 4.30 during this interview.

1364
01:22:48,460 --> 01:22:50,041
[SPEAKER_01]: He was very congenial.

1365
01:22:50,061 --> 01:22:51,882
[SPEAKER_01]: He had to leave about that time.

1366
01:22:51,902 --> 01:22:56,746
[SPEAKER_01]: He wanted to go back out to the ballpark, talk to some more of the players of the various teams.

1367
01:22:56,986 --> 01:22:58,828
[SPEAKER_01]: There were six teams competing in the tournament.

1368
01:23:00,549 --> 01:23:07,495
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, I want to finish up this tape with some of the jokes he told Thursday night at the banquet.

1369
01:23:08,309 --> 01:23:13,133
[SPEAKER_01]: and which he didn't play on the tape here.

1370
01:23:13,153 --> 01:23:17,156
[SPEAKER_01]: He said I ruined the Joe DiMaggio for six years.

1371
01:23:17,577 --> 01:23:18,477
[SPEAKER_01]: I made him a star.

1372
01:23:18,497 --> 01:23:21,099
[SPEAKER_01]: They didn't know he could go back on a ball until he played with me.

1373
01:23:23,101 --> 01:23:26,844
[SPEAKER_01]: The secret to my success was good, clean living in a fast, friendly outfield.

1374
01:23:28,445 --> 01:23:29,826
[SPEAKER_01]: I had only one weakness.

1375
01:23:29,926 --> 01:23:31,908
[SPEAKER_01]: I was a hitter, a pitched ball.

1376
01:23:32,649 --> 01:23:36,792
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, I never broke a bat in my life until last winter, and then I ran over it in my garage.

1377
01:23:38,365 --> 01:23:39,526
[SPEAKER_01]: I was a holdout one year.

1378
01:23:39,546 --> 01:23:42,007
[SPEAKER_01]: Hank Greenberg and Jimmy Foxx both sent me letters.

1379
01:23:42,347 --> 01:23:45,628
[SPEAKER_01]: They said, please sign.

1380
01:23:45,708 --> 01:23:48,229
[SPEAKER_01]: Tony Roselli, he was a real one for getting you going.

1381
01:23:48,710 --> 01:23:51,851
[SPEAKER_01]: We talked about his psychology earlier.

1382
01:23:52,531 --> 01:23:59,134
[SPEAKER_01]: He'd be out there at second, chattering stuff like, geez, Gomez, I wish I was batting against you today with some of the stuff you're throwing up there.

1383
01:23:59,654 --> 01:24:01,055
[SPEAKER_01]: And this guy's on my team, too.

1384
01:24:02,290 --> 01:24:08,734
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, I've never been to Cape Kennedy to hear the rockets go off, but they never would have been any louder than those going past me that day.

1385
01:24:09,875 --> 01:24:12,756
[SPEAKER_01]: All of a sudden, Lazari leaves the field and runs into the dugout.

1386
01:24:12,816 --> 01:24:14,918
[SPEAKER_01]: When he comes back out, I ask him what he was doing.

1387
01:24:15,518 --> 01:24:18,820
[SPEAKER_01]: I went in to tell McCarthy to get the Merrymen off the field, Lazari said.

1388
01:24:20,261 --> 01:24:23,863
[SPEAKER_01]: Jimmy Fox hit the longest serve I never hit in Yankee Stadium off me.

1389
01:24:23,883 --> 01:24:31,668
[SPEAKER_01]: If you've ever been there, I'll get the paste in here, look up in the third deck.

1390
01:24:32,233 --> 01:24:35,174
[SPEAKER_01]: In left field, up there near the back row, there's a sign on the seat.

1391
01:24:35,715 --> 01:24:39,516
[SPEAKER_01]: Cleared the fence at the 420 mark, and it's another 60 feet to that chair.

1392
01:24:39,996 --> 01:24:42,398
[SPEAKER_01]: You can walk out there in an hour and a half from home plate.

1393
01:24:43,158 --> 01:24:49,060
[SPEAKER_01]: The last thing you want to hear after a shot like that is the manager ask some stupid question like, what did you throw him?

1394
01:24:49,981 --> 01:24:52,202
[SPEAKER_01]: But sure enough, here he comes walking out to the mound.

1395
01:24:52,742 --> 01:24:53,863
[SPEAKER_01]: What did you throw him, lefty?

1396
01:24:53,903 --> 01:24:54,743
[SPEAKER_01]: McCarthy asked.

1397
01:24:55,343 --> 01:24:59,585
[SPEAKER_01]: The best pitch of my life for the first 60 feet, Gomez quipped.

1398
01:25:00,045 --> 01:25:01,446
[SPEAKER_01]: I had him fooled until he swung.

1399
01:25:02,430 --> 01:25:03,731
[SPEAKER_01]: That remark cost me $100.

1400
01:25:03,771 --> 01:25:08,713
[SPEAKER_01]: They sent me to Binghamton.

1401
01:25:08,733 --> 01:25:11,034
[SPEAKER_01]: This was after the war years.

1402
01:25:11,935 --> 01:25:13,576
[SPEAKER_01]: And he was player manager there.

1403
01:25:13,616 --> 01:25:15,537
[SPEAKER_01]: We clinched last place on opening day.

1404
01:25:16,077 --> 01:25:17,037
[SPEAKER_01]: We had a heck of a streak.

1405
01:25:17,217 --> 01:25:18,518
[SPEAKER_01]: We lost 14th streak.

1406
01:25:18,978 --> 01:25:20,919
[SPEAKER_01]: If we got rained out, we had a victory dinner.

1407
01:25:21,840 --> 01:25:25,442
[SPEAKER_01]: Had a pitcher from Maine who couldn't get the signals down, so we kept it simple.

1408
01:25:25,942 --> 01:25:29,003
[SPEAKER_01]: One finger was a fastball and two fingers was a curve.

1409
01:25:29,784 --> 01:25:32,145
[SPEAKER_01]: The bases were loaded with nobody out.

1410
01:25:33,325 --> 01:25:37,847
[SPEAKER_01]: When I called him in, I told him again, one finger's a fastball and two fingers is a curve.

1411
01:25:38,227 --> 01:25:39,208
[SPEAKER_01]: That's all you'll need.

1412
01:25:39,448 --> 01:25:41,829
[SPEAKER_01]: We won't worry about any other pitches.

1413
01:25:41,849 --> 01:25:45,430
[SPEAKER_01]: His first pitch is in the dirt, goes all the way to the screen and a run scores.

1414
01:25:46,031 --> 01:25:49,492
[SPEAKER_01]: His next three are bouncing all around the plate and my catcher's taking a beating.

1415
01:25:50,393 --> 01:25:55,535
[SPEAKER_01]: Then he strikes out the next batter in three pitches, the next one too, and the next he strikes out the side.

1416
01:25:56,328 --> 01:25:59,410
[SPEAKER_01]: When he comes into the dugout, I ask him, what happened in the first four pitches?

1417
01:26:00,111 --> 01:26:04,694
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm sure glad you called for that two-fingered ball, because I can't control that thing with just one finger.

1418
01:26:06,936 --> 01:26:10,779
[SPEAKER_01]: I got a double once and took a foot lead and got picked off.

1419
01:26:11,860 --> 01:26:17,765
[SPEAKER_01]: When the manager asked me what happened, I said, I'm not used to being out there.

1420
01:26:17,785 --> 01:26:21,748
[SPEAKER_01]: Once I picked a runner off first base.

1421
01:26:21,768 --> 01:26:23,269
[SPEAKER_01]: I picked off the first baseman, too.

1422
01:26:23,749 --> 01:26:25,270
[SPEAKER_01]: I hit him in the chest with my throw.

1423
01:26:25,310 --> 01:26:26,071
[SPEAKER_01]: He yelled, balk.

1424
01:26:28,445 --> 01:26:31,628
[SPEAKER_01]: And then he began his lecture, or his talk, on that Thursday evening.

1425
01:26:31,648 --> 01:26:33,389
[SPEAKER_01]: He paid a nice compliment to me.

1426
01:26:33,409 --> 01:26:42,516
[SPEAKER_01]: He said he'd spent the morning at the ballpark and the afternoon he had a wonderful time at my house, reminiscing and looking at old baseball materials.

1427
01:26:42,576 --> 01:26:48,020
[SPEAKER_01]: I had some old sporting news there, which he had back in the early 30s, where his picture's on the front page.

1428
01:26:48,541 --> 01:26:53,024
[SPEAKER_01]: I looked at some of those, and I showed him my 1927 scrapbook and a few other things.

1429
01:26:53,084 --> 01:26:57,988
[SPEAKER_01]: He made some nice remarks about how much he enjoyed that time here.

1430
01:26:58,637 --> 01:27:02,018
[SPEAKER_01]: And then he said he apologized also for not showing up last year.

1431
01:27:02,038 --> 01:27:07,880
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, he was the guest speaker for the NCAA Division III World Series last year here at Marietta.

1432
01:27:08,560 --> 01:27:14,462
[SPEAKER_01]: But at the last moment, we were in Florida when this happened, he got ill and couldn't make it.

1433
01:27:14,542 --> 01:27:22,025
[SPEAKER_01]: And I got back just the week of the series, and I didn't know he wouldn't be able to make it until I got back from Florida, because I was scheduled to interview him then.

1434
01:27:22,685 --> 01:27:24,146
[SPEAKER_01]: And that had to be postponed a year.

1435
01:27:24,866 --> 01:27:27,487
[SPEAKER_01]: But the reason was he had to undergo a triple

1436
01:27:28,192 --> 01:27:35,616
[SPEAKER_01]: a bypass heart surgery at that time, so he was unable to make it here in 1981.

1437
01:27:35,676 --> 01:27:51,863
[SPEAKER_01]: But when he began his talk on this Thursday night, June 3rd, 1982, in the Sternwheeler room of the Lafayette Hotel in Marietta, he said, I want to apologize for not being here last year, but the last moment I had triple bypass heart surgery.

1438
01:27:51,883 --> 01:27:53,784
[SPEAKER_01]: He said, you know, it's the only triple I've ever got.

1439
01:27:56,603 --> 01:27:57,664
[SPEAKER_01]: So it was a great interview.

1440
01:27:57,684 --> 01:28:04,370
[SPEAKER_01]: He gave a great talk to the people gathered at the banquet.

1441
01:28:05,490 --> 01:28:17,100
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh yes, he says the record is incorrect in the daguerreotypes and other books that have his lifetime records in them.

1442
01:28:17,821 --> 01:28:22,565
[SPEAKER_01]: As far as his birthdate is concerned, it's listed here in the daguerreotypes, born November 26, 1910.

1443
01:28:24,718 --> 01:28:26,098
[SPEAKER_01]: And he says, that's wrong.

1444
01:28:26,238 --> 01:28:26,899
[SPEAKER_01]: It's 1908.

1445
01:28:27,499 --> 01:28:28,939
[SPEAKER_01]: He was two years older.

1446
01:28:28,959 --> 01:28:33,421
[SPEAKER_01]: So he's going to be 74 this November, where I was under the impression he was 70.

1447
01:28:34,181 --> 01:28:39,002
[SPEAKER_01]: He would be 72 this November, so he's actually 73 now.

1448
01:28:40,663 --> 01:28:41,723
[SPEAKER_01]: I guess that's about it.

1449
01:28:42,063 --> 01:28:44,324
[SPEAKER_01]: That winds up the interview.

1450
01:28:44,364 --> 01:28:51,526
[SPEAKER_01]: Not quite an hour and a half, but he took his responsibilities very seriously by going out to the ballpark.

1451
01:28:52,580 --> 01:29:00,885
[SPEAKER_01]: seeing all the managers of the coaches of the six teams that were there, and all the players in the teams, and talking with them.

1452
01:29:01,026 --> 01:29:06,969
[SPEAKER_01]: Actually, he was out there in the morning, and I went over to meet him in the Lafayette late in the morning to plan what we were going to do.

1453
01:29:07,049 --> 01:29:08,570
[SPEAKER_01]: And he didn't get back until about 11.30.

1454
01:29:09,491 --> 01:29:11,792
[SPEAKER_01]: And I had been waiting almost an hour by that time.

1455
01:29:12,753 --> 01:29:17,136
[SPEAKER_01]: And he apologized for his lateness, but said he was out at the ballpark and was going to have to go out again.

1456
01:29:17,792 --> 01:29:21,955
[SPEAKER_01]: And I said, what I would like to do, I'd like to have him for a couple of hours for an interview.

1457
01:29:21,995 --> 01:29:25,457
[SPEAKER_01]: And so he said, well, how about 2.30 this afternoon?

1458
01:29:25,497 --> 01:29:36,785
[SPEAKER_01]: So I came back over 2.30 in the afternoon, picked him up, and brought him over to the house here for the interview, and then drove him back to the hotel after the interview was over.

1459
01:29:36,805 --> 01:29:38,386
[SPEAKER_01]: I think he went out the ballpark again.

1460
01:29:39,087 --> 01:29:46,272
[SPEAKER_01]: And I did talk to him and his friend Al later that evening, just before the banquet, when we were gathering in the stern of the room.

1461
01:29:47,410 --> 01:29:50,214
[SPEAKER_01]: A very good interview, although I think it could have been a bit longer.

